    "knowledge_point": "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators",
    "problem": "$\\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{1}{3(1+z)}=$",
    "worked_solution": "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $3x(1+z),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $3$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{1}{3(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{x}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{3x(1+z)}+\\frac{x}{3x(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6+x}{3x(1+z)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
